Write a TypeScript module (strict mode, Node 24, `lib` set to `esnext`) that exports `createPool<T>(items: readonly T[])`, a pool of reusable items whose leases are released with an `await using` declaration:

```ts
{
  await using lease = await pool.acquire();
  use(lease.value);
} // the item goes back to the pool here, even if the block throws
```

The returned pool object has:

- `acquire()` -- returns a promise of a lease for a free item. When no item is free, the promise stays pending until one is released; pending acquires are served first-come-first-served, and a released item goes directly to the oldest waiter.
- `available` -- the number of free items (a read-only property); `waiting` -- the number of pending acquires.

A lease has a readonly `value: T` and releases its item through the ASYNC disposal protocol (so it works with `await using`). Releasing a lease a second time does nothing, even if its item has since been leased to someone else. Answer with a single ```ts code block.
